  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rational\nWe introduce a multi-start local search by diversifying the discrete state space through a basis formation technique. The core idea is to generate multiple candidate bases from the parent solution and optimize each independently. This prevents premature local convergence by systematically exploring different trajectory cones. The approach leverages the following distinct properties:\n- Bounded perturbations that maintain satisfaction of \u2211h\u1d62 = n_points/4\n- Forward and backward similarity metrics for structured moves\n- Adaptive basis expansion with boundary control\n\n2. Mathematical Specifications\nThe representation is defined by:\n- A trajectory basis vector T \u2208 [0,1]\u207f with sum(T)=m\n- Maximum move sizes \u0393\u1d62 \u2208 {0.1,0.2,...} for each i\n- Overlap evaluation based on correlation with trajectory-shifted differences\n\nThe optimization problem is:\nMinimize max\u2096 \u2211\u1d62 h\u1d62(1-h\u1d62+k) \nSubject to: \n(1) 0\u2264h\u1d62\u22641 for all i\n(2) \u2211h\u1d62 = n_points/4\n\nThe new formulation adds basis constraints through \u2113\u2081-regularization.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\na. Initialization:\n- Copy parent to active_candidates\n- For each candidate, apply 2-4 small basis transformations via:\n  h\u2080\u1d62 \u2190 h\u1d62 + D\u1d62 (clipped and repaired)\n\nb. Iteration:\n- For each candidate:\n  1. Propose local moves using basis vectors\n  2. Apply constrained optimization with repair\n  3. Track best solution from candidate's neighborhood\n\nc. Move Generation:\n- Used non-local moves similar to previous plan but:\n  - Maintains basis alignment at each search level\n  - Forces gradient cancellation relative to visited states\n\nd. Repair and Tracking:\n- Uses projected gradient for constraints\n- Maintains separate historical states per candidate\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\nStandard tuning parameters plus:\n- Basis expansion factors \u03b2 = [1,1.5,2,...]\n- Convergence monitoring with nested breakers\n- Dynamic priority shifting based on basis quality\n\n5. Budget and Robustness\nPrimarily three-phase radius scaling:\n- Initial exploration: base=C30_attack\n- Growth phase: base=move_window_ratio\n- Optimization: base=gp_move_window_ratio\nEach phase tracks best solution and \n  - Time: budget_start + 3*log(level)\n  - Distance: function-based move size scaling\n\n6. Validation and Returns\nAfter each optimize call:\n- Check feasibility: sum(h) \u2208 [n_points/4\u00b1tol]\n- Compute c5 via correlation*sample_width\n- Return best_solution, best_c5, solutions_count\n\n<c5_bound_crit> acknowledges the need for peer review of interpolation behavior</c5_bound_crit>",
